Usage:
  ugreenctl led list
  ugreenctl led status [TARGET ...]
  ugreenctl led set TARGET ... [--color COLOR] [--brightness VALUE] [--mode MODE]
  ugreenctl led on|off TARGET ...
  ugreenctl led identify TARGET [--seconds 1..60]
  ugreenctl disk list|map
  ugreenctl disk locate /dev/sdX|serial:SERIAL|slotN [--seconds 1..60]

TARGET: power, netdev, netdev2, diskN, or all; use led list for capabilities.
COLOR: blue, red, green, white, yellow, cyan, magenta, purple, orange,
       black, '#RRGGBB', or R,G,B for RGB; palette LEDs also accept
       white-orange, none, channel1/channel2/both when listed.
       Quote hex colors in a shell.
VALUE: raw hardware range or 0%..100% of that range. MODE: on/off/blink/breath
       where supported by the target.
Blink/breath: --on-ms N --off-ms N (default 500/500; total 1..65535 ms).
Target-specific minimum durations are shown by led list --json.

--json, --apply, --force can appear before or after the command.
Writes are previews until --apply; experimental hardware also needs --force.
N76: --n76-direct BUS:0x31 (or BUS:0x26) selects explicit userspace I2C.
     Exact DH2600/DXP480T Plus only; no scan, module build or loading.
     Set requires explicit mode/color; output/PWM/timing remain UNVERIFIED.
     Default without this option retains the bound-driver sysfs path.
--force never bypasses DMI, MCU identity, driver ownership, or lock checks.
Identify restores the complete original LED state after its time limit
or SIGINT/SIGTERM. Restoration is best effort if communication fails.
Disk mapping uses physical PCI/ATA topology, never sdX enumeration order.
Unverified mappings remain unknown; locate refuses them. Calibrated physical
maps are read from /etc/ugreenctl/disk-map.conf (or --map-file PATH).
Calibration files must be root-owned and not group/world-writable:
  model=DXP4800 Plus
  slot1 topology=pci-0000:00:17.0-ata-1
Record only physical slot/topology pairs confirmed on your machine.
